challenges.description:
Why does this compile in TypeScript when it would fail in Java or C#?

  interface Point { x: number; y: number }
  interface Coordinate { x: number; y: number }

  function drawPoint(p: Point): void {}

  const coord: Coordinate = { x: 1, y: 2 }
  drawPoint(coord)  // ← works?

Why does one of these fail but the other doesn't?

  interface Config { host: string; port: number }

  // Case A:
  const c: Config = { host: 'localhost', port: 8080, extra: true }  // ← ?

  // Case B:
  const obj = { host: 'localhost', port: 8080, extra: true }
  const c2: Config = obj  // ← ?

What constraint does an index signature impose, and why does this cause a compile error?

  interface StringMap {
    [key: string]: string
    count: number  // ← error?
  }

What is the difference between annotation and `satisfies`?

  // Annotation — widened
  const colors1: Record<string, string> = { red: '#ff0000', blue: '#0000ff' }
  colors1.red  // type: string

  // satisfies — narrow preserved
  const colors2 = { red: '#ff0000', blue: '#0000ff' } satisfies Record<string, string>
  colors2.red  // type: ?
